# GNU awk 3.0.6, compiled by the tcc this chain built, against ntlibc.
#
# The PE32 counterpart of ../../../linux/bootstrap/gawk/mes-build.sh, which
# drives gawk's own ./configure under bash and then make.  There is neither
# here: the answers are asserted in config.h, as grep, sed, gzip and tar
# assert theirs, and kaem names the sixteen compiles and the one link
# directly.  See the head of default.nix for why.
#
# ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}, not ${TMPDIR}: wine drops TMPDIR on the way into the
# Windows environment, so it arrives empty and cd is left with no argument.

cd ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}

${bin_ungz} --file ${tarball} --output gawk.tar
${bin_untar} --file gawk.tar

cd ${NIX_BUILD_TOP}/gawk-${version}

# One patch, and it is not about the C library.  gawk_name() strips the
# leading path off argv[0], which the NT loader hands over as a full
# backslashed store path; ispath() and isdirpunct() are the same character
# set in the two other places gawk assumes '/'.  See the patch's own header.
#
# The Linux build's no-stamp.patch is NOT carried over.  That patch deletes a
# `date > stamp-h' from configure, so that two builds of the same source do
# not differ by a timestamp.  Nothing here runs configure, so there is no
# stamp-h and nothing to make reproducible.
${patch} -Np0 -i ${patchProgramName}

# What ./configure would have written.  It lands in the tree root, where -I.
# finds it, and awk.h includes it under HAVE_CONFIG_H.
${bin_cp} ${configH} config.h

# gawk 3.0.6 is a flat tree -- every source file is in the root -- so one -I
# list serves the lot and there is no ordering question of the kind tar had.
# gawk's own directory comes first all the same, and one header there has to
# win:
#
#   regex.h  ntlibc grew a <regex.h> and a POSIX regcomp/regexec in the pin
#            this package is built against.  gawk must not use it.  awk.h
#            reaches into `struct re_pattern_buffer' and `struct re_registers'
#            directly (RESTART, REEND, SUBPATSTART, SUBPATEND are macros over
#            regs.start[] and regs.end[]), and re.c calls re_compile_pattern,
#            re_search and re_set_syntax -- the GNU interface underneath the
#            POSIX one, which ntlibc does not have and which is where gawk's
#            regex syntax bits (RE_SYNTAX_AWK and the interval, backslash and
#            character-class flags gawk toggles for --posix and --re-interval)
#            live.  So regex.c below is compiled and gawk's regex.h is the one
#            on the include path.  Because regex.o is a plain object and not
#            an archive member, its regcomp/regexec/regfree satisfy those
#            names before libc.a is consulted at all, and ntlibc's copies are
#            never pulled in -- a choice, not a collision.  Checked with nm.
#
#   getopt.h gawk's declares the same struct option as ntlibc's -- same four
#            members, same order, same three has_arg constants -- so gawk's
#            header still describes what it links against.  Only main.c
#            includes it, and it is included quoted.
#
# One -D, and no more: HAVE_CONFIG_H, which is what makes awk.h, regex.c and
# dfa.c include <config.h> at all.  Everything else configure would have put
# on the command line is in config.h instead, because kaem runs a command
# line without a shell and DEFPATH's value contains quotes.
#
# gawk's own Makefile.in also passes -DGAWK on every compile, and this build
# does NOT.  Nothing in the 3.0.6 tree reads it -- the string only appears in
# copyright headers and in one comment in random.c -- and that was checked by
# building the whole thing both ways: the two gawk.exe are byte for byte the
# same file.  It becomes a real switch in gawk 3.1, where dfa.c uses it to
# take gawk's fatal() and xmalloc(); here it would be cargo.
#
# _WIN32 is left alone, siding with grep, gzip and tar rather than with
# coreutils: the string does not appear anywhere in the gawk 3.0.6 sources.
# What gawk actually branches on is bare WIN32 (three places) and MSDOS, and
# this tcc defines neither -- measured, not assumed.  That is the answer this
# port wants.  gawkmisc.c and io.c both switch on
# `MSDOS || OS2 || WIN32', and taking that branch would mean gawkmisc.pc
# (drive letters, 8.3 names, <io.h>, setmode) and PIPES_SIMULATED, which
# replaces gawk's fork/exec pipelines with temporary files.  ntlibc has fork,
# popen, pclose, waitpid and system, so the POSIX branch is the true one.
alias CC="${tcc} -c -I. -nostdinc -I${ntlibcSrc}/include -I${ntlibcSrc}/arch/i386 -I${ntlibcSrc}/arch/generic -I${ntlibc}/include -B ${ntlibc}/lib -DHAVE_CONFIG_H"

# The twelve files of gawk proper, as Makefile.in's AWKOBJS names them.
CC -o array.o array.c
CC -o builtin.o builtin.c
CC -o eval.o eval.c
CC -o field.o field.c
CC -o gawkmisc.o gawkmisc.c
CC -o io.o io.c
CC -o main.o main.c
CC -o missing.o missing.c
CC -o msg.o msg.c
CC -o node.o node.c
CC -o re.o re.c
CC -o version.o version.c

# The parser.  awk.y is bison input and there is no bison in this chain --
# nor will there be until long after gawk exists, since bison's own configure
# needs an awk.  The 3.0.6 release tarball ships awktab.c, the pre-generated
# output of `bison -y awk.y' (Bison 1.25, 1996), so it is compiled as an
# ordinary C file and awk.y is never looked at.  Had it not shipped, this
# package could not exist at this point in the chain at all -- it is the one
# thing about gawk 3.0.6 that had to be checked before anything else.
CC -o awktab.o awktab.c

# And three of the five files Makefile.in's LIBOBJS names.
#
#   regex.c   gawk's own GNU regex.  See the -I note above for why ntlibc's
#             is not used.
#   dfa.c     the deterministic first pass gawk runs before the backtracking
#             matcher.  It has no counterpart in any C library.
#   random.c  gawk ships its own random() on purpose, and random.h renames it
#             to gawk_random() so that it cannot collide with the library's.
#             ntlibc has random(), srandom() and initstate(), but using them
#             would make `rand()' in an awk program produce a different
#             sequence here than on the Linux half of this bootstrap; gawk's
#             own comment in builtin.c says the same thing.  So this is the
#             one file kept for reproducibility rather than for necessity,
#             and thanks to the renaming it is not even a shadowing.
#
# The two NOT compiled are getopt.c and getopt1.c.  ntlibc has getopt,
# getopt_long and getopt_long_only, and its struct option is gawk's struct
# option member for member -- so main.c's getopt_long call and its optarg,
# optind, opterr and optopt all resolve into libc.a.  This is grep's decision
# and the same one for the same reason.
#
# alloca.c is not compiled either: it is Makefile.in's $(ALLOCA), which
# configure leaves empty when the system has alloca, and ntlibc does.
CC -o regex.o regex.c
CC -o dfa.o dfa.c
CC -o random.o random.c

# One link.  A PE-target tcc adds neither a startup object nor a default C
# library, and the default it would reach for (msvcrt, kernel32) is not what
# this bootstrap has, so crt1.o and -lc/-lntdll are named outright --
# ntdll.def being the import library ntlibc resolves its kernel calls
# through.  -lc -lntdll come last, after gawk's own objects, which is what a
# linker that resolves archives in one pass needs.
#
# There is no -lm.  gawk's configure looks for one because eval.c and
# builtin.c want fmod, pow, sin, cos, atan2, log, sqrt and int; ntlibc keeps
# all of those in libc.a.
${tcc} -B ${ntlibc}/lib -nostdlib ${ntlibc}/lib/crt1.o -o gawk.exe array.o builtin.o eval.o field.o gawkmisc.o io.o main.o missing.o msg.o node.o re.o version.o awktab.o regex.o dfa.o random.o -L ${ntlibc}/lib -lc -lntdll

# Run it before installing it, on awk's real job rather than on --version.
# kaem is --strict, so a non-zero exit from any of these fails the build.
#
# The self-test is written as an awk program that checks its own answers and
# exits 1 if any of them is wrong, because kaem has no pipelines, no
# comparisons and no conditionals -- the checking has to happen inside the
# program being checked.  Between them its checks cover the things a configure
# script actually does with awk: field splitting under a non-default FS, an
# END block with an accumulator, associative arrays and `for (k in a)',
# regexp match, sub/gsub, printf, and NR/FNR across more than one input file.
${bin_cp} ${selfTest} selftest.awk
${bin_cp} ${selfTestData} data1.txt
${bin_cp} ${selfTestData2} data2.txt
./gawk.exe -F : -f selftest.awk data1.txt data2.txt

# It lands as gawk.exe, as tar, bash, make, patch, sed and grep land as
# name.exe -- ntlibc's PATH search tries the bare name before name.exe, so a
# configure script asking for `gawk' still finds it.
#
# awk.exe is a COPY rather than a symlink, for two reasons that both hold:
# the store path is read-only by the time anything would follow the link, and
# NT's symbolic links need a privilege this build does not have and a
# reparse point ntlibc does not create (see gnutar's nt-no-symlinks patch).
# The Linux package copies for the first reason alone.
${bin_mkdir} -p ${out}/bin
${bin_cp} gawk.exe ${out}/bin/gawk.exe
${bin_cp} gawk.exe ${out}/bin/awk.exe
